Running Fusion 360 through a compatibility layer like Wine or Proton is notoriously tricky, and the black canvas is one of the most common graphical bugs. Since you've already tried hiding the navigation bar and switching graphics engines, the issue is likely tied to how the UI frameworks (QT and Chromium) interact with the Vulkan translation layer.

Here are the most effective fixes reported by the Linux Fusion 360 community:

## Detach the UI Panels (Floating Mode)

The browser tree (left side) and comments tab frequently conflict with the DXVK renderer. Try clicking and dragging these tabs to detach them from the main dock. Placing these panels into "floating mode" often forces the renderer to correct itself, immediately clearing the black screen.

## Force a Canvas Redraw

If the canvas turns black specifically after a popup menu appears or a tool is clicked, the Wine environment might just be failing to refresh. Simply pressing the middle or right mouse button and scrolling the wheel slightly can force the engine to redraw the frame and restore your workspace.

## Verify Vulkan 1.3 Support

If you switched the graphics engine to DirectX 11, Wine uses DXVK to translate the graphics calls to Vulkan. DXVK strictly requires a Vulkan 1.3 capable driver. If your Mesa or NVIDIA drivers are out of date (or if your hardware only supports Vulkan 1.2), the translation fails silently and drops you into a black screen. Ensure your system's graphics drivers are fully updated.

## Adjust the QT Rendering Variable

Fusion's UI is built on the QT framework, which sometimes fails to composite correctly over the 3D workspace in Linux. You can alter how QT draws by modifying your launch script or Wine prefix environment variables. Try setting the `QT_OPENGL` variable to either `angle` or `software`.

## Disable Chromium Hardware Acceleration

Fusion relies heavily on an embedded Chromium browser (`adcefwebbrowser.exe`) for UI elements and the data panel, and its hardware acceleration often clashes with Linux graphics wrappers. If you can navigate the menus, go to **Preferences** and disable graphics hardware acceleration.

